            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies the widow as a symbol of vulnerability, defenselessness, and total dependence on God. It also commonly recognizes a corporate, prophetic metaphor where a widow represents a city or nation (especially Israel, Zion, or Babylon) that is desolate, judged, and bereft of its covenant relationship or husband.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the widow specifically as God's covenant people who have been cut off from their Husband.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A socially vulnerable individual under God's protection, or a corporate metaphor for a desolate, abandoned city/nation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"God's covenant people cut off from their Husband (a desolate corporate entity).\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts this specific entry entirely to the corporate/covenantal metaphor (Israel/Zion as a widow), excluding the broader social archetype of individual vulnerability and dependence, which it appears to delegate to a separate entry.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's definition perfectly matches a standard prophetic metaphor identified as a common alternative meaning by the consensus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus recognizes both a primary meaning based on social vulnerability and a secondary, corporate meaning based on prophetic metaphors of desolate cities/nations. The book refines the term by isolating the corporate, covenantal metaphor as the sole definition for this entry.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"It could be argued that omitting the widespread social vulnerability motif from the main 'Widow' entry artificially narrows the biblical symbol, even if a cross-reference points to a related chapter.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
